        v2momMethod: "Slack",
        version: "slack",
        details: {
            overview: "Introduction The Producer and Account Manager at an SMB brokerage jointly own every client account. Today, their coordination is broken. The Producer is in the field - driving between meetings, at client sites, on the phone. The Account Manager is at the desk - processing endorsements, handling certificate requests, fielding carrier notices. They communicate through email, phone calls, and text messages - all disconnected from the account context in the AMS. The result: renewal notices get buried in shared inboxes. Claims are filed and the producer doesn't know until the client calls. Post-meeting follow-ups are emailed and forgotten. The two people who should be most in sync on every account are coordinating through the least structured channels available. This epic introduces Slack as the account-level collaboration surface for Producer and Account Manager teams. Each client account gets a dedicated Salesforce Channel in Slack, where automated alerts (renewals, claims, policy changes) surface alongside team conversation and - for agencies that purchase Agentforce - AI-powered agents that can answer questions, generate briefs, and capture actions. The Core Problem The Producer and Account Manager are out of sync on their shared accounts because: Information is scattered - renewal notices in email, claims in the AMS, tasks in Salesforce, conversations in text messages The AMS is not a communication tool - it is a system of record that agents open when needed, not an always-on…",
            keyFeatures: [],
            impact: ""
        },
